config/saas.txt: the fallback origin and the www rule, stranded by a mid-flight merge

## Why this exists separately

#246 merged at head `83d9a26` while two further commits were being pushed to the same branch, so
`config/saas-arrangement` still carries them and `main` does not. Nothing was lost and nothing was
deleted — the branch outlived the merge because this repo keeps branches — but `main`'s copy of
`config/saas.txt` is 127 lines where the branch's is 163, and the missing 36 lines are the part an
operator reads *before* touching the dashboard. Cherry-picked onto current `main`, byte-identical to
what the stranded branch held.

## What the two commits say

**`73d223b` — the fallback origin is one record and one field, and one per zone.** Checked against
Cloudflare's pages: a proxied `A`/`AAAA`/`CNAME` in this zone plus one dashboard field, and grey
cloud will not do because the whole mechanism is Cloudflare being in the path to read the `Host`
header. The consequence is the load-bearing part — **there is one fallback origin per zone, shared
by every custom hostname.** Pointed at a single Pages project, every consumer domain gets that one
site: correct for one consumer, wrong for two. So consumer two needs the fallback origin to be a
*router* rather than a site, which is the assets-only Worker `docs/flooring.md` already names as its
second answer to serving a name it was never told about. Two open questions, one answer.

Also records that the standalone-zone case is documented: a zone's hostname priority takes
precedence over a *wildcard* custom hostname and the resolution is to register hostnames
individually — so an individual custom hostname for a domain that is also a zone here is the
documented path rather than a refusal. The zone still wins while it answers, so the sequencing
constraint stands.

**`962ea2f` — `www` is a test subject, not a name.** The operator does not keep a `www` ("redundant
and ugly"); approving it here was approving a staging subject. Recorded so nothing later proposes
serving consumers at `www.<their-domain>` as a way around the apex problem, which would trade a real
answer for a name she would not have kept and make the toll somebody else's problem.

## Merge qualifications

- [x] cherry-picked clean; no conflict with anything merged in #246
- [x] declaration and documentation only; nothing here provisions anything
- [ ] `config/saas-arrangement` can be deleted once this lands — it holds nothing else
